  <h2>What a QA Audit Covers</h2>
  <p>A QA audit is a systematic review of everything that affects software quality in your organization. It is not a code review and it is not a test execution exercise – it is an assessment of the systems, processes, and practices that determine whether your software ships with the quality your users expect.</p>
  <p>The audit covers six domains:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Process:</strong> How is testing integrated into the sprint? When do QA activities happen relative to development? Is there a defined defect triage process? What are the entry and exit criteria for testing phases? What gets documented and when?</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling:</strong> What tools are in use for test management, defect tracking, test automation, CI/CD, and monitoring? Are the tools appropriate for the team's needs? Are they being used effectively?</li>
    <li><strong>Coverage:</strong> What percentage of the application's critical paths have defined test cases? What percentage of those test cases are automated? Where are the coverage gaps relative to the application's risk profile?</li>
    <li><strong>Documentation:</strong> Is the test case library current? Do test plans accurately reflect what gets tested? Is there a test strategy document? Are defect reports complete enough to be actionable?</li>
    <li><strong>Team skills:</strong> Does the QA team have the technical skills to build and maintain automation? Are developers involved in testing at the unit level? Is there a shared understanding of quality standards?</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting:</strong> What quality metrics are tracked? Are they reported to the right stakeholders? Do the metrics tell an accurate story about release risk?</li>
  </ul>

