  <div class="faq-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How long does a QA audit take?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">A standard goGreenlit QA audit takes two weeks: three to four days of discovery (interviews, document review, artifact analysis), three to four days of independent analysis and findings development, and two to three days of report writing and prioritization, followed by a findings presentation session.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What does a QA audit report include?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">Executive summary with the three most important findings, detailed findings organized by domain, severity rating for each finding, recommended actions, coverage gap analysis, and a prioritized 90-day improvement roadmap with 10-15 specific initiatives.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How is a QA audit different from a QA assessment?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">An audit is typically more structured – reviewing specific artifacts, interviewing specific roles, and producing a formal findings report. An assessment might be a lighter-weight conversation-based review. Our engagements are audit-depth regardless of what you call them.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What happens after a QA audit?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">The audit produces a findings report and a prioritized roadmap. From there, most clients either implement the improvements themselves using the roadmap as a guide, or engage us for an implementation phase – process design, tooling changes, or an embedded QA engagement to build the coverage the audit identified as missing.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How much does a QA audit cost?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">We scope QA audits based on team size, codebase complexity, and the breadth of what needs to be reviewed. The best way to get an accurate scope is a 30-minute discovery call. We don't publish fixed prices because the right audit for a 10-person startup is a very different engagement from one for a 100-person engineering organization.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

